  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941829/assets/cdce64bf5d02850bbedc2b89af3b2e7447b733831c09d1955e4a9ee97e82d556.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$",
  "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$ Hence, $c=2.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,4),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+2 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}+2 & =4 \\\\ k+2 & =4 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk-c=2-2=0.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+2.$",
